      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The Kicker"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "COLLECTING DUST. A dinosaur fossil that sat in a drawer at London’s Natural History Museum for nearly a century has been identified as a previously unknown species, Futura reports. Named Invictibellatrix amberina , it’s the first megaraptoran discovered in Europe. The three-to-four-metre-long predator lived around 120 million years ago during the Early Cretaceous period and was closely related to tyrannosaurs. Its skull fragment was acquired by the museum in 1924, having been discovered on the Isle of Wight by amateur paleontologist Reginald Walter Tooley . Researchers say the long-clawed, feathered dinosaur would have hunted smaller dinosaurs, lizards, and mammals.",
